From 97b9fe4393b35ac590a18e5a61ac21e872cb9d95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Pacheco <git@michaelpacheco.org>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 06:21:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(xray): sample a windowless scene capture instead of the
 live framebuffer

Glass currently samples m_renderData.currentFB, which contains every window
already drawn, so a glassed window's backdrop is the composited stack beneath
it and anything repainting in that stack invalidates the glass above. Cost
therefore scales with stack depth.

Add plugin:hyprglass:xray (int, default 0). When set, a
CGlassBackgroundElement queued at RENDER_PRE_WINDOWS captures the scene before
any window draws -- wallpaper, background and bottom layers only -- and glass
samples that capture. Every window on a monitor then shares one backdrop and
stack depth stops mattering. The element is queued from the render.stage
handler while the deferred pass is being built; the GL blit runs in draw(),
after those layers have actually rendered.

The capture is monitor-level and invalidated only by monitor, layer, workspace
and config events plus background/bottom layer surface commits, never by
window motion, mirroring how Hyprland keeps its own preblur framebuffer cheap.
The per-surface commit watchers exist because wallpaper daemons repaint the
background layer with plain commits that raise no EventBus event; this is the
same signal Hyprland's own LayerSurface commit path uses to set m_blurFBDirty.
A render.pre listener damages the monitor when the capture is dirty, mirroring
preRender, so a partial-damage frame cannot blit stale regions into it.

Unlike Hyprland's preblur the capture is not pre-blurred, so blur_strength and
blur_iterations keep their meaning. A monitor with no capture yet falls back
to the live framebuffer. Lua: hg.config({ xray = true }) works, booleans
coerce to Hyprlang ints.
